            <!-- Classic Shaded LaTeX Abstract Box -->
            <section id="abstract" class="sheet-abstract">
              <p>
                <strong>Abstract.</strong> Biologically inspired mechanisms such as hard-isolated parallel processing streams and local synaptic plasticity are widely considered incompatible with modern GPU accelerators compared to end-to-end global backpropagation (Global BP). Across five falsification-driven research iterations (v1&ndash;v5), we refute this assumption both formally in <strong>Lean 4</strong> and empirically on <strong>Kaggle NVIDIA T4&times;2 silicon</strong>. We introduce <strong>SPRINT-LP</strong> (<em>Stream-Packed Residual Information-Preserving Native Tensor Local Plasticity</em>), uniting pure cuBLAS batched GEMM stream packing (<code>torch.baddbmm</code>), whole-step <code>torch.cuda.CUDAGraph</code> capture with static FP16 scaling and fused AdamW, and a bio-faithful <em>Shared V1/V2 Stem + Late Bifurcation + Deep-MAN++</em> topology. Our experiments establish four decisive results: (1) on a <strong>single GPU with 100% identical parameters</strong> (<em>L</em> = 12, <em>M</em> = 3, <em>d</em> = 128), increasing local partitioning from <em>K</em> = 1 (Global BP) to <em>K</em> = 6 reduces peak activation VRAM by <strong>79.0%</strong> (758.1 &rarr; 159.2 MB) while running <strong>11.5% faster</strong> (54.71 &rarr; 48.43 ms/step) via L2 cache locality; (2) at matched representation width (Iso-Width <em>D</em> = 768), <em>M</em> = 4 parallel streams achieve <strong>0.79&times; latency</strong> and <strong>1.28&times; energy efficiency</strong> (up to <strong>0.73&times; latency</strong> and <strong>1.39&times; samples/Joule</strong> at <em>d</em> = 256); (3) on <strong>2-GPU Kaggle T4&times;2</strong> with identical parameters, our <em>Zero-Bubble Async Local Pipeline</em> delivers a <strong>2.47&times; real wall-clock speedup</strong> (16.96 vs. 41.82 ms/step); and (4) on parameter-matched natural image multi-prediction (<code>AffCIFAR-10 v2</code>, ~1.26M vs. 1.31M parameters), live 2-GPU async training incurs <strong>zero accuracy degradation</strong> (77.58% vs. 77.19% on 1 GPU), <strong>outperforms Global BP on variant pose prediction</strong> (+0.325 vs. +0.318 <em>R</em><sup>2</sup>; 15.3&deg; vs. 16.2&deg; angular MAE), and achieves clean stream orthogonality (CKA = 0.057).
              </p>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 1 -->
            <h2 id="sec1"><span class="sec-num">1</span> Introduction and Five-Iteration Falsification Trajectory</h2>
            <p>
              Modern deep learning relies predominantly on homogeneous backbones trained end-to-end with global backpropagation. When forced to optimize conflicting <em>multi-prediction</em> objectives simultaneously&mdash;such as class-level <em>invariance</em> alongside continuous geometric pose <em>variance</em>&mdash;shared parameters suffer destructive gradient interference (cos(&nabla;<em>L</em><sub>inv</sub>, &nabla;<em>L</em><sub>var</sub>) &lt; 0) <a class="sheet-cite" href="#ref1">[1]</a> and feature superposition. In contrast, the primate visual cortex shares early orientation and contour processing in V1/V2 before bifurcating into anatomically segregated Ventral (&ldquo;what&rdquo;, invariant) and Dorsal (&ldquo;where/how&rdquo;, variant) streams coordinated via lateral modulation <a class="sheet-cite" href="#ref2">[2,</a><a class="sheet-cite" href="#ref3">3]</a>.
            </p>
            <p>
              Translating parallel streams and local plasticity onto silicon GPUs has historically failed wall-clock and memory criteria due to four hardware mismatches: (i) Python stream loops or narrow grouped convolutions (<code>groups=M</code>) causing cuDNN kernel fragmentation; (ii) iterative settling loops (20&ndash;50&times; slower) in energy-based predictive coding; (iii) heavy pixel-reconstruction auxiliary decoders <a class="sheet-cite" href="#ref4">[4]</a> that erase activation VRAM savings; and (iv) deferred global <code>.backward()</code> calls or per-block <code>GradScaler</code> host-device synchronizations.
            </p>
            <p>
              Rather than relying on unverified intuition, the Enemites Research Team executed five consecutive falsification-first empirical iterations (v1&ndash;v5) on Kaggle NVIDIA T4 GPUs (Turing <code>sm_75</code>, 40 SMs, 65 TFLOPS FP16 Tensor Core, 320 GB/s GDDR6, 70 W TDP) <a class="sheet-cite" href="#ref10">[10]</a>, rejecting every component that failed strict hardware or learning kill criteria (Table 1).
            </p>

            <p class="sheet-caption">Table 1: Five-iteration falsification trajectory on Kaggle NVIDIA T4 / T4&times;2 silicon.</p>
            <div class="sheet-table-scroll">
              <table class="booktabs">
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>Ver.</th><th>Tested Formulation</th><th>Empirical Finding / Falsification</th><th>Engineering Pivot</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td><strong>v1</strong></td><td>Packed <code>bmm</code> with 48&times; <code>.permute()</code> copies; <code>layer_scale=10<sup>&minus;2</sup></code>.</td><td>Saved 56.6% VRAM (passed <em>B</em> = 2048 where Par-GlobalBP OOMed), but 2.20&times; slower; <code>SynGeo</code> stalled at 11%.</td><td>Eliminate <code>.permute()</code>; Kaiming init + <code>layer_scale=0.5</code>.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td><strong>v2</strong></td><td><code>channels_last Conv2d(groups=3)</code> + 4D spatial FiLM (8 ep SMOKE).</td><td><code>NoVICReg</code> hit <strong>89.2% Acc / 0.788 <em>R</em><sup>2</sup></strong> (beating Global BP 68.7% / 0.341), but 3.07&times; slower (<code>groups=3</code> + 4D FiLM).</td><td>Replace 4D FiLM with Pooled FiLM (&lt; 1 ms); lower VICReg to 0.01.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td><strong>v3</strong></td><td>Full 25/35-epoch validation; pixel-0 split (<em>D</em> = [32..128]&times;3), disjoint <em>K</em> = 4.</td><td><strong>Falsified on Tier 2 FULL:</strong> 63.5% vs. 77.0% Global BP (&minus;7.3% pixel-0 capacity starvation + &minus;6.2% <em>K</em> = 4 myopia); 71.3 ms at <em>B</em> = 128.</td><td>Pure <code>torch.baddbmm</code>, Whole-Step CUDA Graph, Shared V1/V2 Stem, MAN++.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td><strong>v4</strong></td><td>GPU Kernel Lab: CUDA Graph + Static FP16 + NVML Energy + T4&times;2 + MAN++.</td><td><strong>Beat Global BP on GPU:</strong> 0.73&times; latency &amp; 1.39&times; samp/J at <em>d</em> = 256; <strong>2.41&times;</strong> on T4&times;2; accuracy jumped +10.3% (73.8% at 726K params).</td><td>Match inference params (~1.3M), sweep <em>M, K</em>, add 4 counterfactuals.</td></tr>
                  <tr><td><strong>v5</strong></td><td><strong>Unified Undeniable Proof:</strong> <em>M</em> &isin; &#123;1..4&#125;, <em>K</em> &isin; &#123;1..6&#125;, CF-1&ndash;CF-4, 2-GPU real data.</td><td><strong>1 GPU identical (<em>K</em> = 6): &minus;79% VRAM &amp; 11.5% faster!</strong> <em>M</em> = 4: 0.79&times; / 1.28&times; energy; T4&times;2: <strong>2.47&times;</strong>; 2-GPU data: zero acc loss &amp; beat Global BP Pose <em>R</em><sup>2</sup>.</td><td><strong>Confirmed</strong> (formal Lean 4 + empirical T4&times;2 proof).</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>

            <!-- Section 2 -->
            <h2 id="sec2"><span class="sec-num">2</span> Formal Mathematical Foundations (Verified in Lean 4)</h2>
            <p>
              All core isolation, memory, roofline, and multi-GPU pipeline properties are formally proved in pure Lean 4 (<code>Init</code> core, zero external Mathlib dependencies, zero <code>sorry</code>) and cross-verified symbolically in SymPy.
            </p>

            <div class="sheet-theorem">
              <p>
                <strong>Theorem 1</strong> (Exact Block-Diagonal Gradient Isolation &amp; Non-Zero Forward Modulation). <em>Consider M &ge; 2 post-bifurcation streams with disjoint parameter blocks &theta; = (&theta;<sup>(1)</sup>, &hellip;, &theta;<sup>(M)</sup>) and block transition h<sub>k</sub><sup>(i)</sup> = F<sub>k</sub><sup>(i)</sup>(h<sub>k&minus;1</sub><sup>(i)</sup>, &theta;<sub>k</sub><sup>(i)</sup>, &#123;sg(h<sub>k&minus;1</sub><sup>(r)</sup>)&#125;<sub>r&ne;i</sub>), where sg(x) = x forward and &part;sg(x)/&part;u = 0 backward. Then for all i &ne; j and every block k:</em>
              </p>
              <div class="sheet-equation">
                <span class="sheet-equation__expr">
                  &part;<em>L</em><sub><em>k</em></sub><sup>(<em>i</em>)</sup> / &part;<em>&theta;</em><sup>(<em>j</em>)</sup> &equiv; 0, &emsp; &lang;<em>g</em><sup>(<em>i</em>)</sup>, <em>g</em><sup>(<em>j</em>)</sup>&rang; &equiv; 0, &emsp; <em>I</em>(<em>H</em><sub><em>k</em></sub><sup>(<em>i</em>)</sup> ; <em>H</em><sub><em>k</em>&minus;1</sub><sup>(<em>j</em>)</sup> | <em>H</em><sub><em>k</em>&minus;1</sub><sup>(<em>i</em>)</sup>, <em>&theta;</em><sub><em>k</em></sub><sup>(<em>i</em>)</sup>) &gt; 0
                </span>
                <span class="sheet-equation__num">(1)</span>
              </div>
              <p>
                <em>eliminating destructive cross-stream gradient conflict (&lang;g<sup>(i)</sup>, g<sup>(j)</sup>&rang; &lt; 0) while preserving non-zero forward cross-stream contextual modulation.</em>
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="sheet-theorem">
              <p>
                <strong>Theorem 2</strong> (Peak Activation Memory <em>O</em>(<em>L</em>/<em>K</em>) &amp; Iso-Width Roofline Speedup Law). <em>(2A) Partitioning an L-layer network into K &ge; 2 eager local-plasticity blocks with immediate graph release yields M<sub>local</sub>(L, K) = (1/K) M<sub>global</sub>(L) + M<sub>aux</sub> &lt; M<sub>global</sub>(L) if and only if M<sub>aux</sub> &lt; ((K&minus;1)/K) M<sub>global</sub>(L). (2B) Packing total width C = M &middot; D into M &ge; 2 dense <code>torch.bmm</code> streams <a class="sheet-cite" href="#ref13">[13]</a> over BN tokens with b-byte elements satisfies:</em>
              </p>
              <div class="sheet-equation">
                <span class="sheet-equation__expr">
                  FLOPs<sub>par</sub> = (1 / <em>M</em>) FLOPs<sub>hom</sub>, &emsp; Bytes<sub>par</sub> &lt; Bytes<sub>hom</sub>
                </span>
                <span class="sheet-equation__num">(2)</span>
              </div>
              <p>
                <em>On a GPU with ridge point R<sub>GPU</sub> = &Pi;<sub>peak</sub> / &beta;<sub>mem</sub>, whenever 2BND &ge; b R<sub>GPU</sub>(2BN + D), both kernels are compute-bound and strict wall-clock speedup T<sub>par</sub> + T<sub>ovh</sub> &lt; T<sub>hom</sub> holds iff T<sub>ovh</sub> &lt; ((M&minus;1)/M) T<sub>hom</sub>.</em>
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="sheet-theorem">
              <p>
                <strong>Theorem 3</strong> (Zero-Bubble Asynchronous Local Pipeline Dominance). <em>Across P &ge; 2 GPUs and m &ge; 1 microbatches, backward-locked GPipe <a class="sheet-cite" href="#ref6">[6]</a> achieves utilization U<sub>GPipe</sub>(P, m) = m / (P &minus; 1 + m). Removing cross-stage backward dependencies via local plasticity <a class="sheet-cite" href="#ref5">[5]</a> eliminates pipeline bubbles, yielding steady-state utilization U<sub>local</sub>(&epsilon;<sub>comm</sub>) = 1 / (1 + &epsilon;<sub>comm</sub>), which strictly exceeds GPipe iff &epsilon;<sub>comm</sub> &lt; (P &minus; 1) / m.</em>
              </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Section 3 -->
            <h2 id="sec3"><span class="sec-num">3</span> SPRINT-LP v5: GPU-Native &amp; Bio-Faithful Unified Architecture</h2>
            <p>
              To bridge hardware speed and natural-image representation quality, <strong>SPRINT-LP v5</strong> combines four systems and neuro-inspired primitives (Figure 1):
            </p>
            <ol>
              <li><strong>Pure Tensor-Core Stream Packing (<code>torch.baddbmm</code>):</strong> Replaces slow <code>Conv2d(groups=M)</code> kernels with contiguous batched GEMMs (<code>cublasGemmStridedBatchedEx</code>) <a class="sheet-cite" href="#ref11">[11]</a> over [<em>M, B&middot;N, d</em>] tensors aligned to multiples of 8/64.</li>
              <li><strong>Whole-Step <code>torch.cuda.CUDAGraph</code> + Static FP16 + Fused AdamW:</strong> Captures all <em>K</em> local blocks&rsquo; forward, loss, backward, <code>_foreach_mul_</code> static descaling (<em>S</em> = 256.0), gradient clipping, and fused capturable AdamW <a class="sheet-cite" href="#ref14">[14,</a><a class="sheet-cite" href="#ref15">15]</a> into a single CPU launch (~30 &mu;s), eliminating 4&times; dynamic <code>GradScaler</code> host-device syncs.</li>
              <li><strong>Bio-Faithful Shared V1/V2 Stem + Late Bifurcation:</strong> Stage 1 (16&times;16, <em>C</em> = 96) uses dense <code>groups=1</code> convolutions to capture synergistic early edges without &radic;<em>M</em> bandwidth inflation, before bifurcating at Stage 2 (8&times;8) and Stage 3 (4&times;4) into hard-isolated Ventral/Dorsal <code>torch.baddbmm</code> streams coupled by stop-gradient <strong>CG-LatFiLM</strong>.</li>
              <li><strong>2-Unit Deep-MAN++ Anti-Myopia + Zero-Bubble T4&times;2 Pipeline:</strong> Each early block trains through a gradient-free EMA shadow (<em>&alpha;</em> = 0.995, updated via <code>_foreach_lerp_</code>) of the full downstream block <a class="sheet-cite" href="#ref8">[8]</a> paired with detached <em>Shared-Output Readout</em> <a class="sheet-cite" href="#ref9">[9]</a>. On 2 GPUs, GPU 0 (Blocks 1&ndash;2) and GPU 1 (Block 3) run 100% concurrently via ping-pong double buffers and non-blocking CUDA events.</li>
            </ol>

