            <!-- Section 4 -->
            <h2 id="sec4"><span class="sec-num">4</span> Empirical Validation &amp; Counterfactual Falsification on Kaggle T4&times;2</h2>

            <h3 id="sec4-1"><span class="sec-num">4.1</span> Single-GPU Bonus Discovery: L2 Cache Locality Beats Global BP at 100% Identical Parameters</h3>
            <p>
              Evaluating Part I-B (<em>L</em> = 12 <code>PureBMMStreamBlock</code> layers, <em>M</em> = 3, <em>d</em> = 128, <strong>100% identical model and parameter count</strong> on a single T4), partitioning from <em>K</em> = 1 (Global BP) to <em>K</em> = 6 (Local BP) not only follows Theorem 2A by cutting peak activation VRAM by <strong>79.0%</strong> (758.1 &rarr; 159.2 MB), but also <strong>monotonically accelerates wall-clock speed by 11.5%</strong> (54.71 &rarr; 48.43 ms/step; Table 2). Because <em>K</em> = 6 executes backward immediately after 2 layers, intermediate activations remain hot in the T4&rsquo;s 4 MB L2 cache instead of spilling to 320 GB/s GDDR6 as in <em>K</em> = 1.
            </p>

            <p class="sheet-caption">Table 2: Single-GPU block-partition sweep (<em>L</em> = 12, <em>M</em> = 3, <em>d</em> = 128, 100% identical parameters) and Iso-Width stream scaling (<em>D</em> = 768, NVML hardware energy counters <a class="sheet-cite" href="#ref12">[12]</a>).</p>
