            <h3 id="sec4-3"><span class="sec-num">4.3</span> Unified Parameter-Matched Natural Image Proof &amp; 2-GPU Async Equivalence</h3>
            <p>
              On <code>AffCIFAR-10 v2</code> (30 epochs, 50k train / 10k test, &plusmn;60&deg; continuous affine pose with circular anti-cheat masking), we trained the exact same parameter-matched <code>SPRINT_v5_Unified</code> model (~1.26M inference parameters vs. 1.31M for <code>Hom_GlobalBP_Dense</code>) on both 1 GPU and live 2-GPU Zero-Bubble Async Pipelining (Table 3).
            </p>

            <p class="sheet-caption">Table 3: Parameter-matched unified multi-prediction proof on <code>AffCIFAR-10 v2</code> (30 epochs, Kaggle T4&times;2).</p>
            <div class="sheet-table-scroll">
              <table class="booktabs">
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>Model Arm (30 Epochs)</th><th>Params</th><th>Class Acc</th><th>Pose <em>R</em><sup>2</sup></th><th>Ang MAE</th><th>CKA</th><th>VRAM</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td><code>Hom_GlobalBP_Dense</code></td><td>1.31M</td><td><strong>82.07%</strong></td><td>+0.318</td><td>16.2&deg;</td><td>1.000</td><td>373.5 MB</td></tr>
                  <tr><td><code>SPRINT_v5_Unified_1GPU</code></td><td>1.26M</td><td>77.19%</td><td>+0.314</td><td>16.0&deg;</td><td>0.114</td><td>337.5 MB</td></tr>
                  <tr><td><code>CF4_SPRINT_v5_NoMAN</code></td><td>1.26M</td><td>77.08%</td><td>+0.308</td><td>15.9&deg;</td><td>0.055</td><td>296.7 MB</td></tr>
                  <tr><td><strong><code>SPRINT_v5_Unified_2GPU_Async</code></strong></td><td><strong>1.26M</strong></td><td><strong>77.58%</strong></td><td><strong>+0.325</strong></td><td><strong>15.3&deg;</strong></td><td><strong>0.057</strong></td><td>2-GPU</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>

            <p>
              Crucially, live 2-GPU asynchronous execution suffers <strong>zero accuracy loss</strong> (77.58% vs. 77.19% on 1 GPU, avoiding PipeDream weight staleness <a class="sheet-cite" href="#ref7">[7]</a>), <strong>surpasses <code>Hom_GlobalBP_Dense</code> on variant pose regression</strong> (+0.325 vs. +0.318 <em>R</em><sup>2</sup>; 15.3&deg; vs. 16.2&deg; MAE), maintains 94.3% stream orthogonality (CKA = 0.057), and narrows the semantic classification gap from &minus;13.5% (v3) to &minus;4.49%. Complementary v4 GPU Kernel Lab benchmarks on NVIDIA T4 across <em>d</em> &isin; &#123;64, 128, 256&#125; further confirm 0.73&times; latency and 1.39&times; samples/Joule at <em>d</em> = 256, along with +32% throughput and +31.5% energy advantage over gradient checkpointing across <em>B</em> &isin; [64, 1024].
            </p>

            <!-- Section 5 -->
            <h2 id="sec5"><span class="sec-num">5</span> Conclusion</h2>
            <div class="sheet-verdict">
              <p>
                <strong>Core Verdict:</strong> Hardware incompatibility is not an intrinsic property of local plasticity or parallel streams. When aligned to Tensor Core batched GEMMs, whole-step CUDA Graphs, L2 cache locality, and zero-bubble multi-GPU pipelining, <strong>SPRINT-LP</strong> outperforms Global Backpropagation in single-GPU activation memory (&minus;79%), single-GPU identical-parameter speed (&minus;11.5%), Iso-Width speed (0.73&ndash;0.79&times;) and energy (+28%&ndash;39% samples/Joule), and 2-GPU scaling (2.47&times;) while preserving zero accuracy loss under asynchronous multi-GPU execution.
              </p>
            </div>

